{"event":"result","result":{"conversation_id":"b854ef22-6415-4308-b71c-1ee2e621ef29","status":"SUCCESS","response":"The issue has been resolved.\n\n### What Changed\nIn [`gen.py`](file:///tmp/h-5HNiTJ/gen.py#L10-L13), changed the lower bound field in `hr_target()` from `\"value\"` to `\"start\"`:\n\n```python\ndef hr_target(step):\n    \"\"\"HR corridor for a step, as a percentage of the athlete's LTHR.\"\"\"\n    lo, hi = step[\"hr_pct\"]\n    return {\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}\n```\n\n### Why\nAs detailed in [`notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md`](file:///tmp/h-5HNiTJ/notes/exact-hr-corridors-on-garmin-from-intervals-icu-api-workouts.md) and [`readback/watch-notes.md`](file:///tmp/h-5HNiTJ/readback/watch-notes.md):\n- When using `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"value\": lo, \"end\": hi}`, Intervals.icu treats the target as a Lap-HR / zone-mapped step during FIT export, which causes Garmin watches to clamp the target boundaries to their own locally stored zones.\n- Using `{\"units\": \"%lthr\", \"start\": lo, \"end\": hi}` designates the target as an Instant-HR corridor. Intervals.icu evaluates the percentage against the athlete's configured LTHR (e.g., 172 bpm) and exports the exact corridor to the watch drift-free.\n\nThe changes and explanation have been documented in [`ANSWER.md`](file:///tmp/h-5HNiTJ/ANSWER.md).\n","duration_seconds":60.366905,"num_turns":1,"usage":{"input_tokens":117266,"output_tokens":1715,"thinking_tokens":0,"cache_read_tokens":127769,"total_tokens":118981}}}
